                                  INTRODUCTION










                             Description of C-Index

          C-Index is a productivity tool written in the C language.  It
          is a  complete implementation  of a B-Tree Indexed Sequential
          Access Method  (ISAM) with extended capabilities for variable
          length data handling. You as the C programmer can use C-Index
          to quickly  create and maintain multi-keyed records stored in
          a single index/data file or to utilize customized indexing of
          data stored  in separate  files. Specific features of C-Index
          include:

          . Variable length keys and data
          . Fully Automated Multi-key storage
          . True Multi-User capabilities with file and record locking
          . Implementation of advanced B-Link structure
          . Single and multiple-keyed access to data
          . Addition and Deletion of keys in any order
          . Full source code
          . Data integrity functions
          . No royalties on developed applications

          The philosophy  of C-Index  is to  provide a  tool that is as
          flexible as  possible. The  system does  not make assumptions
          about how you may choose to store data. Rather, the functions
          allow you  to create  a developmental  environment that  will
          best suit your application.


                               System Requirements

          Because C-Index is a portable C language package, you will be
          able to  use it with a wide variety of compilers. C-Index was
          written using  C syntax  that is compatible with both K&R and
          ANSI standards,  and should  move easily from one compiler to
          another.


                                          Introduction   3
         _________________________________________________


                                   Supplied Materials

              C-Index is  supplied on  5.25" or  3.5" IBM  PC format floppy
              disk.  The source files can be transferred to other platforms
              for compilation  outside the  PC  environment.    Because  of
              constant changes  in the computers and compilers supported an
              up-to-date list  is provided on the release disk.  We make an
              effort to support all the popular platforms and compilers.


                                  How to Use the Manual

              C-Index has  two groups  of function calls: high level multi-
              key functions  and low  level single-key functions.  For both
              of these  groups there  are additional  multi-user functions.
              This manual first describes all high level functions and then
              discusses all low level functions.

              The multi-key  functions are the high level calls of C-Index,
              and are  at the  beginning of  the manual.   For most typical
              data management applications, the multi-key functions are the
              ones you will be using.

              The single-key  functions are  more flexible  and potentially
              more  powerful  but  also  require  more  work  to  implement
              traditional record  filing schemes. They can be easier to use
              for certain applications however, and therefore should not be
              ignored. Again,  single-key functions are discussed after the
              multi-key functions.

              Each set of calls has two chapters:  one describing the usage
              of the  functions and  one  summarizing  the  functions.  The
              chapters  describing  usage  should  be  used  to  learn  the
              functions, and  the summarizing  chapters, to  reference  the
              functions.

              Important: Whichever  set of  functions you  choose  to  use,
              universal information  on both  is contained  in  Chapter  1,
              "C-Index  Universal  Information".  This  chapter  should  be
              thoroughly read  and understood  before proceeding. It should
              also be  consulted for  basic information  about all  C-Index
              functions. The  individual chapters  on the  functions assume
              you have read and absorbed Chapter 1.

              This manual  also contains  additional chapters  on  advanced
              usage, multi-user  support, and  other related  subjects.  Be
              sure to  review these  chapters so you will better understand
              C-Index and its potential uses for your software development.


