                               The C-Index License

          C-Index is sold as a development tool for use on a single
          programmer.  The Individual Copy License (the standard
          "shrink wrap license" supplied with each copy of the product)
          permits the package to be used by a single programmer on a
          single machine. The license agreement permits the
          distribution of applications created with C-Index without
          payment of royalties, provided that the C-Index software is
          distributed in such a way that access to C-Index for program
          development is not possible by the end user. The source code
          for C-Index may not be distributed.  A copy of the license
          agreement is included in this document.  For a thorough
          understanding of the terms of the license agreement we
          encourage you to review it with your attorney.






                                        CHAPTER 1

                              C-INDEX UNIVERSAL INFORMATION







              This chapter  is a  general guide to using C-Index. It covers
              basic ISAM  concepts and  specific terminology  that will  be
              used throughout  the manual. It provides a basis for learning
              and using  all the  functions of  C-Index. Once you have read
              this chapter,  you will be able to decide which functions you
              wish to  learn next. It is important, however, that you fully
              absorb the material presented here to use C-Index correctly.


                                      ISAM Concepts

              Indexed Sequential  Access  Method  (ISAM)  has  become  very
              popular  for   use  in   many  business  systems.  It  allows
              information to  be retrieved randomly by data value (indexed)
              and in  sorted order  (sequential) using just one index file.
              This dual ability distinguishes ISAM from other methods, such
              as Hashing.

              An ISAM  file is  a file  composed of  individual  pieces  of
              information  called   "keys".  A   key  is  an  ASCII  string
              representing some  value in  a  data  record.  The  index  is
              arranged in  such a  way that  keys  can  be  retrieved  both
              randomly and  sequentially, together  with  their  associated
              data record numbers.

              This manual assumes that the programmer is familiar with data
              management techniques.  If you  are not  familiar  with  this
              subject,  we   suggest  that   your  consult   the  following
              references in addition to this manual:

              1: Knuth, Donald E. The Art of Computer Programming, Vol 3,
              Sorting and Searching, Addison-Wesly Publishing Co. 1973

              2: Comer, Douglas. The ubiquitous B-Tree, references,
              Computing Surveys, Vol. 1, No. 2, June 1979


     6                                            
     _________________________________________________


                               C-Index Source Code

          The C-Index  package is  written in  the C  language  and  is
          designed to  function as a library containing data management
          functions callable  from an  application program. Source code
          is supplied  to users  for  documentation  and  customization
          purposes. The source is not required for using C-Index unless
          you have  a compiler  that is  not  directly  supported.  For
          instructions on making libraries for non-supported compilers,
          see Chapter 11, "Source Code Documentation".


                            Interfacing with C-Index

          C-Index is  a library of C language functions that are linked
          with the  application program.  There are no requirements for
          loading memory  resident software  in advance  of running any
          application that  uses C-Index.  The required portions of the
          C-Index system are incorporated in the application program by
          the linking  processing. The  following sections describe the
          steps necessary for calling C-Index functions.


                                  Include Files

          Every program  that calls  C-Index must  #include one  of two
          special  header  files.  One  module  (source  file)  of  the
          application must include the header file cindex.h:

             #include "cindex.h"   /* C-Index/PC */

             or

             #include "cndx.h"     /* C-Index/II */


                           Defining Number of Buffers

          C-Index allows  the definition of the number of buffers to be
          used. Since  C-Index does  its own buffering, it is important
          to be  able to  set the  level of trade-off between speed and
          memory  usage.   In  C-Index/PC  the  number  of  buffers  is
          controlled by  the  define  statement  in  the  header  file,
          cindex.h:

             #define BUFFERS 10

          where 10 is the number of buffers to be used.

          In C-Index/II  the number  of buffers  is controlled  by  the
          global  variable   "bcbnum"  or   by  calling   the  function
          cibufnum(number_of_buffers).


              Chapter 1  C-Index Universal Information   7
         _________________________________________________


              Since each  buffer is 1K, the ability to choose the number of
              buffers can  be very  useful. In  practice  10  buffers  have
              proven to  be a  good general  figure and that is the default
              number specified by C-Index.

              If the program will be executing on computers with sufficient
              memory, more  buffers may  speed up  C-Index  operation.  You
              should experiment  to find the optimum setting.  Depending on
              the compiler  and the  memory model  selected, the  number of
              buffers may  be limited  by the amount of global static space
              available (C-Index/PC) or the global heap space (C-Index/II).


                               Communicating with C-Index

              C-Index consists  of a  set of functions that are linked into
              the application  program. The  functions can  open, close, or
              create a  file, and  add, delete, and find entries in a file,
              in addition to other operations.

              All function  calls to  C-Index must  supply a  pointer to  a
              structure of  type CFILE  (called a  psp, parameter structure
              pointer) which  holds important information about the file on
              which the  function is  operating. On  opening or  creating a
              file, a  pointer is  passed to  a structure of type CFILE and
              subsequent operations  on that open file will also supply the
              same pointer. For instance the statement

                 CFILE datafile;

              creates a  parameter structure  called datafile  that can  be
              subsequently used  for communication  with C-Index.  For more
              detail regarding  how the  psp  must  be  used,  consult  the
              reference sections of the manual which describe the functions
              of interest.


                            Linking C-Index with Applications

              Included in  the C-Index package are libraries containing all
              the functions  discussed in this manual. C-Index is linked in
              the same  way as  any library.


     8                                            
     _________________________________________________


                                 Opening a File

          A file  must be opened for access before any functions can be
          called. The functions for creating a new file will also leave
          it open  for  access.  There  are  various  open  and  create
          functions. For  more detail,  consult the  individual  manual
          sections describing these functions.


                              Calling the Functions

          Every function  returns an  integer return  code. There  is a
          list of  all  return  codes  in  "Appendix  A",  as  well  as
          individual  returns   described  for  each  function  in  the
          summaries. Always  check the  return code, as any information
          obtained will be invalid if the function failed in any way.


                                  Closing Files

          As stated earlier, a file must be opened before it can be ac-
          cessed. It  is just  as important  to close  the file  before
          exiting the program to insure file integrity. C-Index does as
          much as  possible to  take care of this for you, but there is
          no substitute  for closing  a file. The vast majority of user
          problems stem from non-closure of files.


                           Which Function Group to Use

          C-Index has  two distinct groups of function calls: multi-key
          and single-key  functions.  Each  group  has  its  respective
          characteristics and  advantages. The choice of which group to
          use is best determined by the nature of your application.

          For example,  a  typical  application  will  need  to  manage
          information in  the form of records. Each record will contain
          several fields with two or more fields acting as "key" fields
          for retrieving  the record.  For this  type  of  application,
          multi-key functions  will automatically  handle  the  complex
          processes of addition, update and deletion of records.

          Other types  of  applications  may  have  a  requirement  for
          managing information that is not oriented around records. For
          example,  it   would  be   possible  to  use  C-Index  in  an
          application to store and retrieve the definitions of words in
          a dictionary.   The  single-key routines provide a simple and
          fast way  to access  information based  on one key value.  In
          this example  the definition  of a  word in  a dictionary  is
          located using the word itself as the key.


              Chapter 1  C-Index Universal Information   9
         _________________________________________________


                                 The Multi-key Functions

              The  multi-key   functions  are   a  "record"  based  set  of
              functions. They fully automate the process of managing multi-
              keyed data  records. The  application program  specifies  the
              definition of the record and key structure. C-Index then does
              all the  work of  storing and  retrieving these  records. The
              method for defining the record structure has been designed to
              be as flexible as possible. For the majority of applications,
              the multi-key  functions are the best choice. It is suggested
              that you first examine the multi-key functions to see if they
              satisfy your needs.


                                The Single-Key Functions

              The single-key functions operate on objects called "entries".
              Each entry  contains a  variable length key, a record number,
              and variable length data of any format.

              This set of functions is a more universal data storage scheme
              that allows  the storage,  by keyed value, of variable length
              data up  to 16K  of any  format. The multi-key functions were
              built entirely  by using single-key functions. The single-key
              functions may  be seen  as flexible building blocks which can
              be combined and built upon to make any kind of storage system
              desired. They are also very useful for storing such pieces of
              information as  access codes,  system information,  and other
              types of  information  that  are  typically  used  in  system
              development.

              An advanced  usage of  C-Index might  include the  use of the
              multi-key functions  for record  storage and  the  single-key
              functions  for  all  else.  Low  level  file  access  becomes
              unnecessary because  it is handled by C-Index. Thus, portable
              programs are  much easier  to write  using C-Index.  For more
              information, see Chapter 10, "Advanced Usage of C-Index."

              If the  multi-key functions  do not offer enough flexibility,
              the single-key  functions should  be used. And again, because
              the single-key  functions are  the  building  blocks  of  the
              C-Index system,  it is  helpful to  be  familiar  with  their
              general usage.


     10                                           
     _________________________________________________


                                Error Processing

          Each function  returns a  return code  which should always be
          checked. In  any file  system, there are a number of problems
          that can  occur such  as disk  error, out of disk space, etc.
          C-Index internally  handles all these problems correctly, but
          if a program ignores return codes which are reporting errors,
          serious problems can occur. Each function description in this
          manual contains  a section  on return  codes. In  addition, a
          complete list of error codes may be found in "Appendix A".






                                        CHAPTER 2

                              USING THE MULTI-KEY FUNCTIONS









              This chapter  provides a  general discussion of the using the
              Multi-key functions  of C-Index.  You must  read this chapter
              before using  these functions.  The detailed  descriptions of
              each function  will be found in Chapter 3, "Summary of Multi-
              key Functions".


                         Description of the Multi-Key Functions

              The multi-key  functions automate  the  process  of  managing
              multi-keyed records. These functions use a data definition to
              create the keys and data record. The data definition includes
              key descriptions and pointers to each field. The functions do
              all the  work in  building and extracting records, as well as
              error checking during the multi-key operations. The functions
              are easy  to use,  and very  consistent in their syntax. When
              you have  learned to use them effectively, you will find that
              they satisfy  most  of  your  data  management  needs.  If  a
              different data  storage system  than  that  provided  by  the
              multi-key  functions  is  needed  however,  the  lower  level
              single-key functions may be suitable. The multi-key functions
              should be  tried first  however, as  most applications can be
              easily handled  by them.  Features of the multi-key functions
              include:

              . A concise set of powerful functions
              . Automatic building of keys and records from data definition
              . Variable length keys and data
              . Storage of Keys and data in same file, reducing open files
              . Full error checking during multi-key adding
              . Fields at any location in memory
              . Data integrity with checksum techniques
              . Multiple record types allowed in same file
              . Deleted record space reclaimed automatically


                       An Example of Usage: A Name and Phone List

              The best  way to  describe how to use the multi-key functions
              is to  provide an  example. We  will progressively  show  the
              basic structure of a program which uses these functions. This
              example is kept extremely simple for clarity.


     12                                           
     _________________________________________________


          The example  program will  maintain a  name and  phone number
          database. The  last name  will be  used as  the key to search
          through the  file. The cleanest way to handle data records in
          C is  to define  a structure that represents the data record.
          The structure  can then  be used  to supply  the data  to  be
          added, and  to receive  the data  when reading. The structure
          for this example is:

             struct nap {          /* name and phone data structure */
                char lastname[10]; /* last name, key */
                char firstname[20];          /* first name */
                char phone[20];    /* phone number */
             };

          All the  functions in  the following  examples use  the above
          definition.

          Next we must declare a structure of this type to be used.

             struct nap naprec;

          We now can use naprec for input and output of data records of
          this type.

          In this  example we  use the  last name as the only key. This
          means  that   all  finds   will  use   a  name  to  find  the
          corresponding data  record. In C-Index, all keys are standard
          C language  zero terminated  strings, that  is, a  series  of
          characters ending  with a final byte of binary zero. We could
          also use the first name or last name and first name for keys.
          This would  allow us to search the file by last name or first
          name.

          Note: See  the example program, example.c, on the source code
          disk for an example of writing multi-keyed access routines.


                                  The Datalist

          A datalist  is a  description of  the data record to be used.
          The purpose  of the  datalist is  to provide  all the  needed
          information for  data record  reading, writing, and deleting.
          The datalist  defines which  fields in a record will be keys,
          and what  type the  fields and  keys  are.  It  also  defines
          maximum field lengths, and pointers to each field. Typically,
          it mirrors  the definition  of the  data  structure  for  the
          record in  memory, although  it  is  not  required  that  the
          individual fields  of a record all be a part of the same data
          structure. In  fact, fields may be stored anywhere in memory,
          as indicated  by the  pointer to  the field  defined  in  the
          datalist.


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  13
         _________________________________________________


              Physically, a  datalist  is  an  array  of  structures,  each
              structure being a description of the individual fields and/or
              keys in  the data record. The individual structure is of type
              FIELD and its definition looks like this:

                 struct flddef {
                    char fldtype;      /* type of data in field */
                    char keytype;      /* type of key */
                    char dupflag;      /* duplicate or unique key */
                    char fldlen;       /* maximum length of field */
                    char fldindex;     /* index (key) number */
                    char *fldptr;      /* pointer to (& of) field */
                 };

                 typedef struct flddef FIELD;

                                   Creating a Datalist

              To declare  a datalist for a data record with 4 fields (as in
              our example), the statement

                 FIELD naplist[4];

              creates an array of 4 FIELDs. We use one more than the number
              of data  fields because the last FIELD is used for specifying
              the end. This is accomplished by specifying:

                 naplist[3].fldtype = ENDLIST;

              and will be explained in more detail later.


                                Initializing the Datalist

              Once the  datalist has  been created,  it must be initialized
              with the  appropriate values  in order  to correctly describe
              the data  record. The  simplest method is to use one function
              which is called at the beginning of the program. The function
              to initialize could appear as follows:


     14                                           
     _________________________________________________


             struct nap naprec;    /* globally defined */
             FIELD naplist[4];

             initdlist()           /* initialize nap list */
             {
                /* initialize last name field */
                naplist[0].fldtype = STRING;
                naplist[0].keytype = STRING;
                naplist[0].dupflag = DUPKEY;
                naplist[0].fldlen = 10;
                naplist[0].fldindex = 1;
                naplist[0].fldptr = naprec.lastname;   

                /* initialize first name field */
                naplist[1].fldtype = STRING;
                naplist[1].keytype = NONKEY;
                naplist[1].dupflag = NONKEY;
                naplist[1].fldlen = 20;
                naplist[1].fldindex = 0;
                naplist[1].fldptr = naprec.firstname;

                /* initialize phone field */
                naplist[2].fldtype = STRING;
                naplist[2].keytype = NONKEY;
                naplist[2].dupflag = NONKEY;
                naplist[2].fldlen = 20;
                naplist[2].fldindex = 0;
                naplist[2].fldptr = naprec.phone;

                /* signal end of list */
                naplist[3].fldtype = ENDLIST;
             }

          The following is a brief description of the above example:

          naplist[0].fldtype = STRING;

          Indicates that  the first field is of type string, in C terms
          (sequence of  characters,  zero  terminated).  See  reference
          section for all allowable field types.

          naplist[0].keytype = STRING;

          Declares the  field to  be a  key, and  of type  STRING. Only
          STRING and NONKEY (meaning not a key field) are allowed.

          naplist[0].dupflag = DUPKEY;

          Declares that  duplicate name  keys are allowed. With a field
          that is a unique identifier, use the UNQKEY type.


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  15
         _________________________________________________


              naplist[0].fldlen = 40;

              Indicates that  the maximum length of the field is 40 and  is
              used to  check the  field length  to  make  sure  it  doesn't
              overflow the  field buffer.  This helps  to avoid many common
              and hard to find C errors.

              naplist[0].fldindex = 1;

              Tells C-Index  to  store  the  name  key  in  index  1.  When
              searching for  a record  by name  key (using  dfind) the  key
              number indicates which index of keys to search.

              naplist[0].fldptr = naprec.lastname;

              Tells C-Index  where the  field is (when adding a record) and
              where to put the found field (on reading a data record).

              naplist[1].fldtype = STRING;

              Declares that the second field is type string.

              naplist[1].keytype = NONKEY;

              This declares that the field is a non-key field.

              naplist[1].dupflag = NONKEY;

              Although it is a nonkey field, this statement is required for
              integrity checking.  C-Index always  checks the  datalist for
              valid types  to insure  that each  field has  been  specified
              correctly.

              list[1].fldindex = 0;

              Again, although  this is  a non-key  field, this  command  is
              another check on the definition of the list. Also required.

              naplist[7].fldtype = ENDLIST;

              Signals the  end of  the datalist,  and  the  previous  field
              becomes the  last. You  MUST have  this declaration  in  your
              datalist, or the program will give an invalid datalist error.


     16                                           
     _________________________________________________


                          How C-Index Uses The Datalist

          C-Index asks  for the maximum field length for string fields,
          but only  stores the  actual length  of the  string. Thus, in
          addition to  variable length  fields, C-Index  uses  variable
          length data  records.  In  fact,  only  absolutely  essential
          information is stored in the data record, and null fields (or
          0 fields for numeric types) are not physically stored at all.
          This can  greatly increase  the space  efficiency for storing
          data records. C-Index also stores very little of the datalist
          definition in  the record.  Because  of  this  fact,  C-Index
          requires that  you always use the same datalist for reading a
          record that  was used  for adding  or changing  a  record.  A
          checksum of  the datalist  used on  adding a record is stored
          with the record on disk and this value is checked against the
          supplied datalist on reads. If the checksums do not match, an
          error message is returned on reading the record.


                             The Data Record Buffer

          Before C-Index  adds or  reads a  data record  it must have a
          buffer in which to build the record. On opening or creating a
          file, the program specifies the location and size of a record
          buffer. To  determine the  maximum size  of the  data  buffer
          required for any data structure, the following formula should
          be used:

             maximum_buf_size =
                total_length_fields +
                (number_fields * 2) +
                (number_binary_fields * 2) +
                7;

          This would mean for our example

             len of fields = 10 + 20 + 20 = 50
             number fields * 2 = 6
             number binary fields * 2 = 0 (no BINARY field types)
             50 + 6 + 7 = 63

          Thus, a buffer of at least 63 bytes is necessary. This can be
          accomplished by:

             char workbuf[70];

          Notice that there are 7 extra bytes allocated for workbuf. It
          is advisable  to add  a few  extra bytes  to  compensate  for
          possible miscalculation.  C-Index checks  that  it  does  not
          overflow the  buffer, but  it must  use the  length  that  is
          specified. If  this length is wrong and the buffer overflows,
          the program  will probably malfunction in unpredictable ways.
          If in doubt, be generous in the allocation of buffer space.


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  17
         _________________________________________________


              At this  point, the  main function  of the sample program can
              now be produced:

                 /* example of a name and phone list */

                 #define BUFFERS 10    /* use standard amount */
                 #include "cindex.h"   /* required header */

                 struct nap {
                    char lastname[10]; /* last name, key */
                    char firstname[20];          /* first name */
                    char phone[20];    /* phone number */
                 };

                 struct nap naprec;    /* declare data record */
                 CFILE napfile;         /* psp  */
                 FIELDS naplist[4];    /* our datalist */
                 char workbuf[70];     /* work buffer */

                 void main()
                 {
                    initdlist();       /* see definition above */

                 /* main body of code goes here */

                    exit(0);
                 }

              The above  statements are  all that  is necessary to begin to
              use the file in this example. This preparatory work must only
              be written  once and  it contains all the information C-Index
              must know about your file.  With C-Index, it is not necessary
              to run  a  separate  utility  program  to  define  your  file
              information.


                                   Using the Functions

              The individual  functions can  now be discussed. It should be
              pointed out  that there are 7 basic file operations needed to
              maintain a  C-Index file: create a file, open a file, close a
              file, add  a record,  find a  record by  key, find  a  record
              sequentially, and  delete a  record.  The  C-Index  multi-key
              routines have  exactly one  function call  for each  of these
              operations. (Additional  multi-user versions are discussed in
              Chapter 8,  "Multi-user Support"). C-Index also has an update
              function, which  is a  combination  of  the  delete  and  add
              functions.

              With only  one flexible  function for  each operation,  it is
              simple to access C-Index files. In addition, writing programs
              with  C-Index  multi-key  routines  encourages  a  consistent
              programming sytle.


     18                                           
     _________________________________________________


                      Creating, Opening, and Closing a File

          The three  functions to  create, open  and close  a file are:
          dcreate, dopen, and dclose.

          The dcreate function is defined as follows:

          int dcreate(psp, filename, workbuf, buflen)
          CFILE *psp;         /* pointer to psp */
          char *filename;     /* pointer to name of file to open */
          char *workbuf; /* pointer to work buffer */
          int buflen;         /* length of work buffer */

          The file must not already exist or there will be an error, so
          be sure to remove an existing file before creating a new one.
          The usage for dcreate looks like:

             ret = dcreate(&napfile, "nap.dat", workbuf, 63);

          The file is now open and ready to access.



          The dopen function is defined as follows:

          int dopen(psp, filename, workbuf, buflen)
          CFILE *psp;         /* pointer to psp */
          char *filename;     /* pointer to name of file to open */
          char *workbuf; /* pointer to work buffer */
          int buflen;         /* length of work buffer */

          Remember, in  order  to  open  a  file,  it  must  have  been
          previously created by C-Index. The usage for dopen is:

             int ret;

             ret = dopen(&napfile, "nap.dat", workbuf, 63);

          The variable  ret is  an integer  return code.  All functions
          return  an   integer  and  should  always  be  checked.  (See
          reference  section   for  all   return  codes.)  Notice,  the
          parameter &napfile is used here. The & character must be used
          in this  case in  order to  pass a  pointer to the psp rather
          than to pass the psp itself.

          The dclose function is defined as follows:

          int dclose(psp)
          CFILE *psp;         /* pointer to an open psp */

          A file  must have  been opened before it can be closed. Also,
          if a  psp was  used to  open a file, that file must be closed
          before the  psp can  be used  again to open or create another
          file. The usage for dclose:


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  19
         _________________________________________________


                 ret = dclose(&napfile);


                                     Adding Records

              There is one way to add a multi-key record to a C-Index file,
              using the  dadd function.  The dadd  function is  defined  as
              follows:



              int dadd(psp, datalist)
              CFILE *psp;              /* pointer to an open psp */
              FIELD *datalist;         /* pointer to datalist */

              Remember, the  datalist must  have been  initialized and  the
              file must  have been  opened before  any information  may  be
              added to it. After a routine has completed setting the values
              of a  data record,  dadd may  then be  called.  The  datalist
              specifies the type and location of fields in memory. The dadd
              function uses  the datalist to build a variable length record
              and add  the record  and its associated keys to the file. For
              example, a  function called  getdata() obtains all the fields
              of information  from the  user and  puts them in the name and
              address fields  in the  example program  structure naprec. To
              add a record the following routine could be written:

                 addrec()    /* add a record to the file */
                 {
                    int ret; /* return code from dadd */

                    getdata();         /* first get data */
                    ret = dadd(&napfile, naplist);         /* do add */
                 }

              Note that  the routine  getdata would  only have  to fill the
              naprec data  structure, and  would never change the datalist,
              naplist. Again, once the datalist has been initialized, there
              is no  need to access it. Of course, in the above example the
              return code should be checked for any possible errors.


                                     Finding Records

              C-Index uses  two methods  of finding  records.    These  two
              methods are  referred to as find operations. The two kinds of
              find operations  are: random  and sequential.  To  perform  a
              random find,  the  dfind  function  is  used;  to  perform  a
              sequential function, the dseq function is used.


     20                                           
     _________________________________________________


          The dfind function is defined as follows:

          int dfind(psp, indexnum, key, keytype, findtype)
          CFILE *psp;         /* pointer to open file psp */
          int indexnum;  /* index number to search */
          char *key;          /* value to look for */
          int keytype;        /* key type (only STRING allowed) */
          int findtype;       /* type of find - see below */

          The type  of random  find operation  (findtype) may be one of
          the following:

          EQUAL     find only exact matches
          GREATEQ   find anything greater than or equal to key
          GREAT     find anything greater than key
          LESSEQ    find anything less than or equal to key
          LESS           find anything less than key

          For example,  assuming an entire list of names has been added
          by using  dadd. It  may now  be  desirable  to  find  certain
          records based  on a  name key, in this case, the first record
          with the  last name  SMITH. (Note  that it is important to be
          careful when adding and finding keys because C-Index keys are
          case-sensitive.)

             ret = dfind(&napfile, 1, "SMITH", STRING, EQUAL);

          This statement  is a  request to look in index# 1 (defined by
          the datalist  as the  name key)  for the  first record with a
          last name  of "SMITH". If the find was successful, the return
          code will  be OK. If it is unsuccessful, the return code will
          be FAIL, indicating that no records were found with a name of
          "SMITH". Any  other  return  code  indicates  that  an  error
          occurred.

          As another example of the use of random find, a record may be
          found with a last name that starts with the letter "T".

             ret = dfind(&napfile, 1, "T", STRING, GREATEQ);

          This statement  is a  request to  look in  index# 1  for  any
          record that is equal to or greater than "T". C-Index uses the
          standard ASCII  collating sequence,  and therefore  any  name
          that starts with a "T" (including the name "T") will be equal
          to or greater than "T". A successful return code in this case
          would be  either OK  or GREATER.  OK indicates  that an exact
          match was  found; GREATER  indicates that a greater value was
          found. In  this example,  a return code of GREATER could also
          indicate a record starting with letters higher than "T", such
          as "U" or "Z".

          On the  multiple find  types (GREATEQ,  LESSEQ), C-Index will
          always first  try to find an exact match, and then look for a
          greater or less than match.


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  21
         _________________________________________________


              The other  random find  types in  C-Index may  be used  in  a
              similar manner.

              The second  type of  find operation  in C-Index is sequential
              find (dseq).   It  is used  to locate  records in  sequential
              order. A  sequential find  does not locate using a key value,
              as does  a random find, but rather, from the current position
              in the file. In other words, a sequential find may obtain the
              next key  in sequence,  the previous key, the first key (from
              the beginning) or the last key (from the end).

              The dseq function is defined as follows:

              int dseq(psp, indexnum, seqtype)
              CFILE *psp;         /* pointer to open file */
              int indexnum;  /* index number to read from */
              int seqtype;        /* sequential type */

              The allowed types of sequential finds are:

              NEXT       find next record in sequence
              PREV       find previous record in sequence
              FIRST      find first record in file
              LAST            find last record in file

              To use  the FIRST and LAST sequential find types, no previous
              positioning is needed; they simply retrieve the first or last
              record in  the file. To use the NEXT and PREV sequential find
              types, the  position must  be set  by a  successful  dadd  or
              dfind. For example:

                 ret = dseq(&napfile, 1, FIRST);

              finds the  first record  in the file (i.e., the first name in
              alpha sequence). (Note that if the return code is FAIL, there
              are no records to be found using that index).


     22                                           
     _________________________________________________


                                 Reading Records

          The above  examples are  incomplete however, because there is
          no mention of a datalist in the functions. It might be asked,
          "Without the  datalist, how  can my  record be  read?".  That
          would be  a very  good question, and the answer is "It can't"
          because an  important function  has been  omitted up  to this
          point. Why  not read  the record  in directly on finds, then?
          Remember it is possible to find a record greater than or less
          than desired,  or to finding nothing at all. The exact record
          needed is  not always  found. In addition, there may be times
          when a  key count is desired, and in this case, time is saved
          by not  reading the  data record. C-Index therefore, provides
          the capability  of deciding when to actually read the record.
          The functions  dfind and  dseq prepare  C-Index to  read  the
          record requested,  but the  function dread  must be  used  to
          actually read the data record.

          The dread function is defined as follows:

          int dread(psp, datalist)
          CFILE *psp;              /* pointer to open file */
          FIELD *datalist;         /* pointer to datalist */

          A typical usage:

             ret = dfind(&napfile, 1, "JONES", STRING, EQUAL);
             if (ret == OK)                  /* found something */
                ret = dread(&napfile, naplist);


                                Deleting Records

          Deleting a record is accomplished with one function, ddelete.
          The definition of ddelete is as follows:

          int ddelete(psp, datalist)
          CFILE *psp;              /* pointer to open file */
          FIELD *datalist;         /* pointer to datalist */

          In order  to delete, a current record must have been set by a
          previously successful  dfind  or  dseq.  When  deleting,  the
          datalist  is  required  because  C-Index  must  know  how  to
          construct the  keys before  it is  able to  delete  them.  An
          example usage of ddelete is:

             /* first find name to delete */
             ret = dfind(&napfile, 1, "BAD", STRING, EQUAL);
             if (ret == OK)
                ret = ddelete(&napfile, naplist);
                /* delete on good find */


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  23
         _________________________________________________


              As an elaboration on the above example, it might be desirable
              to call  dread before  deleting, display  the record  on  the
              console and  verify with  the user  if it  really  should  be
              deleted.


                                    Updating Records

              There is  one function  to update  a record. Updating is best
              described as  a single  function  call  to  both  delete  the
              current record  and then  add a new one in its place. First a
              record must  be made  current, then  a new data record built,
              and then dupdate called.

              The definition of dupdate is defined as follows:

              int dupdate(psp, datalist)
              CFILE *psp;
              FIELD *datalist;


     24                                           
     _________________________________________________


          For example, to update the record for "JONES":

             /* find record */
             ret = dfind(&napfile, 1, "JONES", STRING, EQUAL);
             if (ret == OK)
                ret = dread(&napfile, naplist);   /* read record */
             if (ret == OK)
             {
                getdata();         /* get new data for this record */
                ret = dupdate(&napfile, naplist);      /* change it */
             }

          All the  functions needed  for fully  maintaining a  database
          have now been discussed. For more detailed information, refer
          to the  reference section  that follows and study the example
          program  supplied  on  disk.  The  file  "example.exe"  is  a
          executable version  of the  example source  code contained in
          "example.c". Experimenting  with this  program  will  give  a
          sense for how to use the functions that have been discussed.


                                 Advanced Usage

          The  basic  multi-key  functions  discussed  above  are  very
          flexible   and    should   accommodate    most    programming
          requirements.  When   more  flexibility  is  needed  however,
          C-Index  includes  some  additional  capabilities  for  these
          multi-key routines.


                        Accessing the Parameter Structure

          As stated,  the parameter structure (psp) is used to transfer
          information between  the application  program and the C-Index
          functions. In  most cases  the program  need not  examine the
          information in  the psp when using multi-key functions. Those
          situations in  which it might be useful to set or examine the
          psp information are described below.


                      Examining the Current Record Number;

          The data record number that is returned by the dfind and dseq
          functions (the  "current" record  number) is contained in the
          psp long  int variable  currec  (i.e.  psp.currec).  For  our
          previous example,  we might  look at  the record  number like
          this:

             if (dfind(&napfile, "Test", 1, STRING, EQUAL) == OK)
                printf("Current record number = %ld", napfile.currec);


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  25
         _________________________________________________


              It may  be desirable  for the  program to  save  the  current
              record number for reading a data record at a later time. This
              is accomplished  by saving  the current  record  number,  for
              example after  a dfind,  and then  restoring it  in  the  psp
              before calling dread.


                                Examining the Current Key

              The key  that was  found from the dfind and dseq functions is
              pointed to  by the  psp char  * variable key (i.e., psp.key).
              This is  always set  by the locating functions dfind and dseq
              which can  be useful  when using  non-equal finds by dfind or
              sequential operations  with dseq  and deciding whether or not
              to read  the data  record based  on the key found. Again from
              the previous  example, the method for examining the found key
              might be as follows:

                 if (dfind(&napfile,"Test",1,STRING,GREATEQ) == GREATER)
                    printf("Greater key found = %s", napfile.key);

              Note here  that after  dread the  current  key  from  a  find
              operation is  null. This  occurs because  dfind also uses the
              psp, and  in looking  for the data record, it resets the key.
              Therefore, be  sure to  look at  the key  before reading  the
              record. Because the keys are built from the data record, once
              it has  been read  it is  possible to  examine the key in the
              appropriate field of the data record itself.


                                   Multiple Datalists

              There may be occasions when it is necessary to have different
              datalists in  the same  file. Since C-Index always checks the
              supplied datalist against the datalist of the record, it must
              be known  in advance  which  datalist  to  supply  to  dread.
              C-Index has  an additional  piece of information encoded with
              each data record called the idbyte. It is also located in the
              char psp variable psp.idbyte.

              The idbyte is an identifying byte value stored near the front
              of each  data record.  For  instance,  in  the  case  of  two
              datalists, naplist1  and naplist2,  the idbyte  for  naplist1
              could be set to 1 and the idbyte for naplist2 could be set to
              the value  2. The  value for naplist1 could be set in the psp
              before adding or updating records as follows:

                 /* have assembled a record already */
                 napfile.idbyte = 1; /* this record has idbyte of 1 */
                 ret = dadd(&napfile, naplist1);

              The  idbyte  may  then  be  retrieved  by  using  the  dgetid
              function, which has the following definition:


     26                                           
     _________________________________________________


          int dgetid(psp)
          CFILE *psp;

          After a  find and  before reading the record using dread, the
          dgetid function  can be used to read the idbyte. Based on its
          value, the  appropriate datalist  can be used in a subsequent
          dread of  the record. dgetid returns a condition code, and if
          the code  is OK the idbyte will be in the variable idbyte. An
          example of usage is:

             ret = dfind(&napfile, 1, "Jones", STRING, EQUAL);

             if (ret == OK)
                if (dgetid(&napfile) == OK)   /* always check return*/
                   if (napfile.idbyte == 1)
                      dread(&napfile, naplist1);
                   else
                      dread(napfile, naplist2);

          Because this is a more advanced feature, it can be ignored if
          it is  not necessary  to mix  multiple record  types within a
          single-key index.  The idbyte  value will  always be  set  to
          whatever is  in the idbyte variable at the time of add. It is
          meant only  for the  programmer's  reference,  and  does  not
          influence the internal functioning of C-Index.


                       Auto Initialization of the Datalist

          Since the  datalist is  an array,  it can be initialized as a
          static array  at the  top of  the source  code file as is any
          other array.  Initializing an  array of  structures  requires
          thought but  it can  eliminate the coding involved in setting
          up the datalist. For example, the previous example's datalist
          might have been specified as follows:


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  27
         _________________________________________________


                 #include "cindex.h"

                 /* assumes naprec already defined */
                 FIELD naplist[] = {
                 /* fldtype  keytype  duptype  len  index#  fldptr */
                    STRING,  STRING,  DUPKEY,  10,  1, naprec.lastname,
                    STRING,  NONKEY,  NONKEY,  20,  0, naprec.firstname,
                    STRING,  NONKEY,  NONKEY,  20,  0, naprec.phone,
                 LASTFIELD
                 };

              Note: the  comment line  showing the  field types  helps keep
              track of the type being defined.

              The above definition of naplist accomplishes exactly the same
              thing as  the initdlist()  function. With  a large datalist a
              great deal  of  programming  time  can  be  saved  since  the
              compiler does  all the  work. Notice that the macro LASTFIELD
              sets the  appropriate values  for the last item in a datalist
              and is defined in the header file cindexsb.h.

              The main  problem with this approach to defining the datalist
              is that  some compilers  will not  support  this  syntax.  In
              addition, some  compilers will not allow a global variable to
              be initialized  with a pointer cast to a type other than char
              *. Experimentation  is the  best way  to find  out what  each
              compiler allows.

              A good  compromise is  to initialize  the datalist  supplying
              null field  pointers, and  code a function that sets only the
              field pointers.  Refer to  the example.c  program to see this
              method of initializing a datalist.


                       Example Program for the Multi-key Functions

              An example  program is  supplied to  illustrate uses  for the
              multi-key functions.  This is  an interactive program that is
              designed  to  run  only  on  IBM  PC/AT  computers  and  100%
              compatables since  it both  writes to  screen memory and uses
              BIOS calls.

              The example  implements a  simple database  type program that
              allows full screen data entry and display of a record with 10
              fields of  information. The  information is stored with three
              key values.  Using function  keys all the multi-key functions
              can be  performed, including  add, delete,  update, search on
              keyed   value,    and   next/previous/first/last   sequential
              searches.


     28                                           
     _________________________________________________


                       Supplied Files for Example Program

          example.exe Pre-compiled version  of the  program,  ready  to
                      run.

          example.c   Main source  file showing  function calls  to the
                      C-Index routines.  This part  of the program code
                      is transportable  to computer  systems other than
                      the IBM PC.

          scrnio.c    Include  file  containing  source  code  for  the
                      screen handling,  including record  display, data
                      entry, and  function key handling. This file con-
                      tains machine  dependent code  designed  specifi-
                      cally for the IBM PC. To transport the program to
                      other environments  would require  modifying  the
                      keyboard and display handling routines.


                               Datalist Variables

          Setting  the   datalist  correctly  is  absolutely  essential
          because C-Index  must have  an accurate  image of  the record
          that is  being used.  The following  describes each variable,
          its uses, and allowable settings:

          fldtype     The type  of data pointed to by the fldptr value.
                      Valid types are as follows:

                      STRING       zero terminated string
                      BINARY       binary bytes, specify length
                      SHORTINT     short variable
                      INT               integer variable
                      LONG         long variable
                      FLOAT        float variable
                      DOUBLE       double variable
                      ENDLIST      must be last entry in array

                      Be sure  to remember to terminate the datalist by
                      setting the  last entry  (i.e. the entry past the
                      last field description) as the ENDLIST type.

          fldlen      The field  length. In  the case of binary fields,
                      this length  specifies the  exact length  of  the
                      field. In  the case  of string  fields, fldlen is
                      the maximum  size of  the array that contains the
                      string. For  the other field types, this value is
                      ignored.


              Chapter 2  Using the Multi-Key Functions  29
         _________________________________________________


              fldptr      The pointer  to the data field in memory. This is
                          a char  * pointer value to the variable in memory
                          where the data is stored. If the variable pointed
                          to is  not a  char type,  it is necessary to cast
                          the  pointer   when  initializing  the  datalist.
                          Setting this value correctly is the most critical
                          step in  creating the  datalist. Clearly,  if the
                          wrong address is specified, the wrong information
                          will be  used on data write operations and memory
                          will  be  corrupted  when  performing  data  read
                          operations.

              keytype     The type  of key  to be  built from  the data, if
                          any. In  the current  release  of  C-Index,  only
                          string keys are allowed. If the data field is not
                          used as a key field, the NONKEY type must be set.
                          Valid types are as follows:

                          STRING       zero terminated string key
                          NONKEY       not a key value field

              dupflag     The Duplicate/Unique  key  flag.  This  field  is
                          referenced only  when the  field is used as a key
                          field.  When  duplicate  is  specified,  the  key
                          stored may  be duplicate.  When unique  is speci-
                          fied, the  key value  stored must be unique. When
                          using unique  key fields, note that if the key is
                          not unique  when adding or updating the record an
                          error (FAIL) will be returned and the record will
                          not be added or changed. For data fields that are
                          not keys,  this field  is  ignored.  The  dupflag
                          values are as follows:

                          DUPKEY       Duplicate key
                          UNQKEY       Unique key
                          NONKEY       Not a key field

              fldindex    The key  field index  number. This  is the  index
                          number in  which the key is stored when adding or
                          updating the  record. The legal index numbers for
                          keys are 1-15.


                                     Common Problems

              Probably  the  most  common  error  is  an  invalid  datalist
              definition. This is usually caused by omitting some part of a
              field's definition,  or by  giving an  invalid type  for some
              part of  the datalist. To avoid errors, consult the reference
              section on the multi-key routines for all allowable types. In
              addition, be sure to define the last field in the datalist as
              ENDLIST or C-Index will not know where the end occurs.


     30                                           
     _________________________________________________


          Another error  can occur  if the  pointer  to  the  field  is
          incorrect. On  dread, C-Index  stores the  found data  in the
          areas pointed  to by the datalist. If the pointers are wrong,
          memory can be corrupted and can cause very serious errors.






                                        CHAPTER 3

                             SUMMARY OF MULTI-KEY FUNCTIONS









              This chapter contains a summary of of the Multi-key functions
              of C-Index. Each one is listed in alphabetical order with its
              name, synopsis,  description, a list of special return codes,
              notes (if  any), a  coding example, and references to related
              functions. For  a more  general  discussion  on  using  these
              functions  refer   to  Chapter   2,  "Using   the   Multi-key
              Functions".


                                      Abbreviations


              abv            usage                    type
              _________________________________________________

              cc             condition code           int
              databuf        data buffer for dopen    char *
              &datafile      address of file psp      CFILE *
              datalist       address of datalist array     FIELD *
              DBUFLEN        buffer length of databuf int
              file           pointer to file name     char *
              ftype          find type                int
              key            pointer to key           char *
              keyn           key-index number         int
              keytype        STRING only              int
              psp            parameter struct pointer      CFILE *
              stype          sequential type          int
              wrkbuf         work buffer for routines char *
              wrklen         work buffer length       int


                                      Return Codes

              All functions  return an  integer  return  code  that  should
              always be checked. There is a complete list of error codes in
              "Appendix A". In the RETURNS section of each function summary
              there is  a list  of returns  that have  a unique meaning for
              that specific  function.  For  example,  all  functions  will
              return DSKERR  if an  error in  a disk  operation occurs.  To
              avoid redundancy  this chapter will only discuss those return
              codes whose significance need special explanation.


     32                                           
     _________________________________________________


          NAME
             dadd - add a data record

          SYNOPSIS
             int dadd(psp, datalist)
             CFILE *psp;           /* file psp */
             FIELD *datalist;      /* record datalist */

          DESCRIPTION
             Adds a  new record  to the  file  specified  by  the  psp.
             Automatically builds a variable length record as specified
             by the  datalist.   Automatically adds the keys to the re-
             quired indexes as specified by the datalist.

          RETURNS
             OK          Successfully added the record.
             FAIL        Duplicate key encountered when adding.

          NOTES
             Care must  be taken  when using  unique keys  to check for
             completion of the add.

             The idbyte  can be  set into  the record  by  setting  the
             psp.idbyte variable for the record just before calling the
             dadd function.  The idbyte can then be read before reading
             the record by using the dgetid function.

          EXAMPLE

             cc = dadd(&datafile, datalist);

          SEE ALSO
             ddelete
             dupdate
             dgetid


             Chapter 3  Summary of Multi-Key Functions  33
         _________________________________________________


              NAME
                 dclose - close a file

              SYNOPSIS
                 int dclose(psp)                 /* Close file */
                 CFILE *psp;           /* file to close */

              DESCRIPTION
                 Closes the  file that was previously opened using dopen or
                 dcreate.

              RETURNS
                 OK          Successfully closed the file.
                 FAIL        Unable to close file (system error).

              EXAMPLE

                 cc = dclose(&datafile);

              SEE ALSO
                 dopen
                 dcreate


     34                                           
     _________________________________________________


          NAME
             dcreate - create a file

          SYNOPSIS
             int dcreate(psp, file, wrkbuf, wrklen)
             CFILE *psp;           /* file psp */
             char *file;           /* file name */
             char *wrkbuf;                   /* work buffer */
             int wrklen;           /* work buf len */

          DESCRIPTION
             Create a  new C-Index  index/data file using the operating
             system file  name specified.  Initializes the file, closes
             it to  ensure the file information is on disk, re-opens it
             and initializes the psp with the work buffer information.

          RETURNS
             OK          Successfully created the file, file is open.
             FAIL        Unable to create file (system error).
             FILEXIST    File already exists, does not do create.


             Chapter 3  Summary of Multi-Key Functions  35
         _________________________________________________


              NOTES
                 Sets the  data integrity  level to  3, maximum  integrity.
                 (See Chapter 6, Data Integrity Features).

                 The size  of the  data buffer is determined by the maximum
                 size of  the data record plus the overhead of the variable
                 length record  mechanism. Be  generous with space to avoid
                 borderline problems.  The formula  for the  minimum buffer
                 size is:

                 bufsiz = (max length of all fields)
                 + (7 bytes overhead)
                 + ( (number of fields) * 2)
                 + ( (number of binary fields) * 2)

                 Refer to  the discussion of the physical record layout for
                 a more detailed discussion.

              EXAMPLE

                 cc = dcreate(&datafile, "exam.fil", databuf, DBUFLEN);

              SEE ALSO
                 dclose
                 dopen


     36                                           
     _________________________________________________


          NAME
             ddelete - delete the current record

          SYNOPSIS
             int ddelete(psp, datalist)
             CFILE *psp;
             FIELD *datalist;

          DESCRIPTION
             Deletes the  current record  in  the  file.  Automatically
             deletes all  related keys  in the  indexes. Deleted record
             number is retained for future use by dadd or dupdate.

          RETURNS
             OK          Record and keys successfully deleted.
             NOCUR       No current record for this file.
             CHKSMERR    Incorrect datalist specified.

          NOTES
             The current record number for this file must have been set
             by a  call to either the dfind or dseq functions, prior to
             the ddelete call.

          EXAMPLE

             cc = ddelete(&datafile, datalist);

          SEE ALSO
             dadd
             dupdate
             dfind
             dseq


             Chapter 3  Summary of Multi-Key Functions  37
         _________________________________________________


              NAME
                 dfind - find a record by key value

              SYNOPSIS
                 int dfind(psp, keyn, key, keytype, ftype)
                 CFILE *psp; /* file psp */
                 int keyn;   /* index # */
                 char *key;  /* key pointer */
                 int keytype;          /* type of key */
                 int ftype;  /* find type */

              DESCRIPTION
                 Finds records according to the key definition and the find
                 "type." For  example, the EQUAL type searches for an equal
                 match to  the key  definition. It  does not  read the data
                 record. The  data record  number is  set as  the "current"
                 record and  returned in  the psp  variable currec. The key
                 actually found  is returned using the psp pointer variable
                 key.

              RETURNS
                 OK          Found and returned specified record.
                 FAIL        No matching key found.
                 GREATER     Higher value key found with GREATEQ.
                 LESSTHAN    Lower value key found with LESSEQ.


     38                                           
     _________________________________________________


          NOTES
             Valid find types:

             EQUAL       Exact key match required.
             GREATEQ     Greater than or equal key.
             GREAT       Next greater value key.
             LESSEQ      Less than or equal key.
             LESS        Next smaller value key.

             Valid key types:

             STRING      String type  of key.  Only the  string type is
                         allowed in the current release.

             A subsequent  dread can  be used to return the data record
             found by a successful dfind.

          EXAMPLE

             cc = dfind(&datafile, 1, "SMITH", STRING, EQUAL);

          SEE ALSO
             dread
             dseq


             Chapter 3  Summary of Multi-Key Functions  39
         _________________________________________________


              NAME
                 dgetid - read idbyte of current record

              SYNOPSIS
                 int dgetid(psp)
                 CFILE *psp; /* file psp */

              DESCRIPTION
                 Read the idbyte of the current record.

              RETURNS
                 The idbyte  value is  returned in  the parameter structure
                 variable psp.idbyte.

                 Condition codes returned:

                 OK          Successfully read the ID Byte.
                 FAIL        Unable to read ID Byte.
                 NOCUR       No current record.

              NOTES
                 The idbyte  is originally  put into  the record by setting
                 the psp.idbyte variable for the record just before calling
                 the dadd function.

              EXAMPLE

                 int idbyte;

                 cc = dgetid(&datafile);
                 idbyte = datafile.idbyte;

              SEE ALSO
                 dadd


     40                                           
     _________________________________________________


          NAME
             dopen - open a file

          SYNOPSIS
             int dopen(psp, file, wrkbuf, wrklen)
             CFILE *psp;           /* file psp         */
             char *file;           /* file name   */
             char *wrkbuf;                   /* work buffer      */
             int wrklen;           /* length of buf    */

          DESCRIPTION
             Open a  preexisting C-Index  index/data  file,  using  the
             specified name and work buffer.

          RETURNS
             OK          Successfully opened file.
             FAIL        Unable to open file (e.g. file not found)

          NOTES
             Sets the  data integrity  level to  3 which is the maximum
             level (See Chapter 6, Data Integrity Features).

             The size  of the  data buffer is determined by the maximum
             size of  the data record plus the overhead of the variable
             length record  mechanism. Be generous! The formula for the
             minimum buffer size is:

             bufsiz = (max length of all fields)
              + (7 bytes overhead)
              + ( (number of fields) * 2)
              + ( (number of binary fields) * 2)

             Refer to  the discussion  of  the  Data  Record  Structure
             (Source  Code   Documentation,  Chapter  11)  for  a  more
             detailed discussion.

          EXAMPLE
             cc = dopen(&datafile, "example.fil", databuf, DBUFLEN);

          SEE ALSO
             dclose
             dcreate


             Chapter 3  Summary of Multi-Key Functions  41
         _________________________________________________


              NAME
                 dread - read the current record into the datalist

              SYNOPSIS
                 int dread(psp, datalist)
                 CFILE *psp;           /* file parameter structure */
                 FIELD *datalist;      /* pointer to datalist array*/

              DESCRIPTION
                 Reads the  current data  record. The  current data  record
                 number must  have been  set from a previous key operations
                 using dfind or dseq.

              RETURNS
                 OK          Successfully read data.
                 FAIL        Error occurred reading the data record.
                 CHKSUMERR   Invalid datalist for reading this record.
                 CHKSUMDERR Data error found reading record.

              NOTES
                 The datalist  supplied must  have the same characteristics
                 defined as  the datalist  used to add or update the record
                 being read.

              EXAMPLE

                 cc = dread(&datafile, datalist);

              SEE ALSO
                 dfind
                 dseq


     42                                           
     _________________________________________________


          NAME
             dseq - find a record in sequence

          SYNOPSIS
             int dseq(psp, keyn, stype)
             CFILE *psp;           /* file psp */
             int keyn;             /* key index number */
             int stype;            /* sequential type */

          DESCRIPTION
             Sequential key  search. Finds  the next,  previous, first,
             and last  sequential key  in an  index. Does  not read the
             data record.  The data  record  number  found  is  set  as
             "current" and  is returned in the psp variable currec. The
             key found is returned using the psp pointer variable key.

          RETURNS
             OK          Found the requested sequential record.
             FAIL        Cannot find requested sequential key.

          NOTES
             Valid sequential types (stype):

             FIRST       First key in index.
             LAST        Last key in index.
             NEXT        Next key.
             PREV        Previous key.

             A subsequent  dread can  be used to return the data record
             found by a successful dseq.

          EXAMPLE

             cc = dseq(&datafile, 1, NEXT);

          SEE ALSO
             dfind
             dread


             Chapter 3  Summary of Multi-Key Functions  43
         _________________________________________________


              NAME
                 dupdate - update the current record

              SYNOPSIS
                 int dupdate(psp, datalist)
                 CFILE *psp;           /* file psp */
                 FIELD *datalist;      /* record datalist */

              DESCRIPTION
                 Updates the  "current" data  record with  the new  values.
                 Automatically deletes  old records  and keys  and adds new
                 record and  keys. The current data record number must have
                 been set by a previous key operation using dfind or dseq.

              RETURNS
                 OK          Successfully updated record.
                 FAIL        Duplicate key found updating the record.
                 BADOLDDEL   Error deleting original keys
                 BADDATDEL   Error deleting original data record.
                 CHKSUMERR   Datalist does not match original record.

              NOTES
                 If the  update fails,  the original  record remains in the
                 file unless  a disk error is encountered trying to restore
                 that record.

              EXAMPLE

                 cc = dupdate(&datafile, datalist);

              SEE ALSO
                 dadd
                 ddelete
                 dfind
                 dseq


         Appendix A  Return Codes
         _________________________________________________


          name                usage

          OK = 0              An entry with an exact match was found.

          GREATER = 2         An entry  was found  where the  key value
                              does not  match and the entry found has a
                              key of the next greater positional value.
                              Only returned  by dfind  when  using  the
                              GREATEQ option.

          LESSTHAN = 3        An entry  was found  where the  key value
                              does not  match and the entry found has a
                              key of the next smaller positional value.
                              Only returned  by dfind  when  using  the
                              LESSEQ option.

          FAIL = -1           The operation  failed. The  exact meaning
                              varies for each function.

          PARMERR = -2        Error in the specified parameters.

          PSPERR = -3         Corrupted, already  open, or unopened psp
                              data structure  (depending on the type of
                              function).

          DSKERR = -4         Disk error occurred during operation.

          BADINDEX = -5       Bad index file header record.

          WRITERR = -6        Disk error while writing buffer.

          SEEKERR = -7        Disk error  while  seeking  for  read  or
                              write of a buffer.

          NOSPACE = -8        No disk  space is  left for the operation
                              (disk full condition).

          OLDERR = -10        Error  opening  old  file  during  cbuild
                              function.

          NEWERR = -11        Error creating  new  file  during  cbuild
                              function.

          INCOMPLETE = -12    Incomplete data  record found in any find
                              function.

          BAD = -13           Error found  in file  using ccheck  func-
                              tion.

          BADDLIST = -14      Invalid information in datalist.

          NOCUR = -16         No current  record number. Must use dfind
                              or dseq to position to a current record.


                              Appendix A  Return Codes  45
         _________________________________________________


              BADOLDDEL = -17     Failed deleting  original  index  entries
                                  during the  dupdate function.  An attempt
                                  is made to restore the original entries.

              DATAERR = -18       An error  occurred while reading, adding,
                                  or updating the data portion of a record.

              BADDATDEL = -19     Failed to  delete old  data record during
                                  ddelete or dupdate.

              CHKSUMERR = -20     Checksum  of  the  data  record  and  the
                                  datalist do  not  match.  This  indicates
                                  that  the  datalist  used  in  adding  or
                                  updating the record and the datalist used
                                  in reading  the record  are  not  defined
                                  having the same characteristics.

              CHKSUMDERR = -21    The checksum  of the  data  values  shows
                                  that  the   record  has  been  corrupted.
                                  Indicates a  hardware failure  (or  power
                                  failure with files left open).

              BADFLDTYP = -22     Invalid  field   type  specified  in  the
                                  datalist.

              BADKEYTYP = -23     Invalid  key   type  specified   in   the
                                  datalist.

              BADDUPFLG = -24     Invalid   duplicate/unique   flag   value
                                  specified in the datalist.

              BADFLDLEN = -25     Invalid length specified in the datalist.

              BADSTRLEN = -26     Invalid string  length, it is longer than
                                  the maximum field length specified in the
                                  datalist (datalist.fldlen).

              BADFLDINX = -27     Invalid index  number specified for a key
                                  field in the datalist.

              BUFOVER = -28       When building  the variable  length  data
                                  record, the  buffer specified on open has
                                  been found  to be smaller than needed for
                                  this particular record.

              FILEXIST = -29      File  requested   by  a  create  function
                                  already exists.  No create  operation  is
                                  performed in this case.

              BADNODE = -30       A  corrupted   node  was   detected  when
                                  searching through  the index. Indicates a
                                  hardware failure  (or power  failure with
                                  files left open).


     46                                           
     _________________________________________________


          FILENOTLOCKED =-101 File is not locked.

          FILELOCKED =-102    File is locked.

          MODEERR = -103      Open mode error.

          RECSHLK = -104      Record is locked by a sharing process.

          RECSFLK = -105      Record is locked by self.

          RECUNLK = -106      Record is unlocked.

          RECNOLK = -107      Record is unlocked and should be locked.


         Appendix B  Alphabetical List of Functions
         _________________________________________________


              Single-Key Functions:

              cbuild - rebuild a file
              cchange - change an entry
              ccheck - check the integrity of a file
              cchgcur - change the current record
              cclose - close an open file
              ccreate - create and leave open a new file
              cdelcur - delete the current entry
              cdelete - delete an entry by key value
              cdupadd - add an entry, allowing duplicate keys
              cfind - find an entry by key value
              cfirst - find the first entry in an index
              cflush - flush C-Index buffers
              cgetcur - get current entry
              clast - get last entry in an index
              cnext - get next entry in an index
              cnextrec - retrieve the next available record number
              copen - open a C-Index file for access
              cprev - get previous entry from an index
              csave - save an entry
              csetfile - set level of data integrity
              csetrec - set next available record number
              cunqadd - add an entry, duplicate keys not allowed

              Multi-Key Functions:

              dadd - add a data record
              dclose - close a file
              dcreate - create a file
              ddelete - delete the current record
              dfind - find a record by key value
              dgetid - read id byte of current record
              dmclose - multi-key close file in multi-user mode
              dmcreate - multi-key create file in multi-user mode
              dmopen - multi-key open in multi-user mode
              dopen - open a file
              dread - read the current record into the datalist
              dseq - find a record in sequence
              dupdate - update the current record

              Multi-User Functions:

              mclose - single key close in multi-user mode
              mclrlock - unlock the current entry
              mclrrec - unlock the current record
              mclrsema - clear semaphore
              mcreate - single entry create in multi-user mode
              msetlock - lock current entry
              msetrec - lock current record
              msetsema - set a semaphore
              mtstlock - test lock status of current entry
              mtstrec- test lock status of current record
              mtstsema - test the status of a semaphore






